{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And HaShem spoke to Moses, saying</b> — Now that HaShem had spoken with Israel face to face, the Ten Commandments, and had commanded them by means of Moses certain commandments that are like fathers to the commandments of the Torah, just as our Sages practiced with the converts who come to convert (Yevamot 47a), and Israel had accepted upon themselves to do everything that He would command them through Moses, and He had made a covenant with them concerning all this — from now on they are to Him for a people and He is to them for Elohim, just as He had stipulated with them from the beginning: \"And now if you will surely obey My voice and keep My covenant, you shall be to Me a treasure\" (Exodus 19:5), and He said: \"And you shall be to Me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation\" (Exodus 19:6) — behold, they are holy and worthy that there should be among them a sanctuary to cause His Shekhinah to dwell in their midst. Therefore He first commanded concerning the matter of the Mishkan, that there should be for Him a house in their midst consecrated to His name, and there He would speak with Moses and command the children of Israel.<br><br>Now the principal purpose of the Mishkan is the place of the resting of the Shekhinah, which is the Ark, as it says (Exodus 25:22): \"And there I will meet with you, and I will speak with you from above the Ark cover.\" Therefore He gave priority to the Ark and the Ark cover here, for it is first in rank, and He juxtaposed to the Ark the Table and the Menorah, for they are vessels like it, and they allude to the purpose of the Mishkan, which was made on their account. But Moses gave priority in the section of <i>Vayakhel</i> to the Mishkan, its tent, and its covering (Exodus 35:11), and so did Bezalel do (Exodus 36:8), because it is proper to give it precedence in the making.<br><br>And the secret of the Mishkan is this: that the Glory that dwelt upon Mount Sinai would dwell upon it in hiddenness. And just as it says there (Exodus 24:16): \"And the glory of YHWH dwelt upon Mount Sinai,\" and it is written (Deuteronomy 5:21): \"For HaShem our Elohim has shown us His glory and His greatness,\" so it is written of the Mishkan: \"And the glory of YHWH filled the Mishkan\" (Exodus 40:34). And Scripture mentions concerning the Mishkan twice: \"And the glory of YHWH filled the Mishkan,\" corresponding to \"His glory and His greatness.\" And the Glory that was seen by them at Mount Sinai was always with Israel in the Mishkan. And when Moses would enter (Exodus 34:34), the word that had been spoken to him at Mount Sinai would come to him. And just as it says concerning the giving of the Torah (Deuteronomy 4:36): \"From heaven He made you hear His voice to instruct you, and upon earth He showed you His great fire,\" so concerning the Mishkan it is written (Numbers 7:89): \"And he heard the voice speaking to him from above the Ark cover, from between the two cherubim, and He spoke to him.\"<br><br>And the repetition of \"and He spoke to him\" is to convey what they said in the tradition: that the voice would come from heaven to Moses from above the Ark cover, and from there He would speak with him. For every word with Moses came from heaven by day and was heard from between the two cherubim, in the manner of \"and His words you heard from the midst of the fire\" (Deuteronomy 4:36), and therefore both of them were gold. And so Scripture says (Exodus 29:42–43): \"where I will meet with you, to speak to you there... and it shall be sanctified by My glory,\" for there would be the appointed place for the word, and it would be sanctified by My glory.<br><br>And whoever looks carefully at the verses spoken concerning the giving of the Torah and understands what we have written about them (see below, verse 21) will understand the secret of the Mishkan and the Temple, and will be able to meditate upon it from what Solomon said in his wisdom in his prayer in the Temple: \"YHWH, Elohim of Israel\" (I Kings 8:23) — just as it says at Mount Sinai: \"And they saw the Elohim of Israel\" (Exodus 24:10). And there he added to explain \"YHWH\" regarding the matter to which we alluded above, for \"the Elohim of Israel who sits upon the cherubim\" (I Kings 8:23), as it says: \"And the glory of the Elohim of Israel was above them, from above — it was the living creature that I saw under the Elohim of Israel by the river Chebar, and I knew that they were cherubim\" (Ezekiel 10:19–20). And David said: \"And the pattern of the chariot, the cherubim of gold spreading out and covering over the Ark of the Covenant of YHWH\" (I Chronicles 28:18). And so he would always invoke in the Temple: \"for the name of YHWH\" (I Kings 5:19), \"for Your name\" (I Kings 8:44), and he would say each time: \"then You hear from heaven\" (I Kings 8:32) — in the attribute of mercy. And it is written (I Kings 8:44–45): \"and they pray toward the city which You have chosen, and the house which I have built for Your name, and You hear from heaven,\" and in explanation he said: \"For will Elohim truly dwell with man upon the earth? Behold, the heaven and the highest heavens cannot contain You\" (II Chronicles 6:18). And it is written concerning the Ark: \"to bring up from there the Ark of the Elohim, upon which the name is called, the name of YHWH of hosts who sits upon the cherubim, which is upon it\" (II Samuel 6:2). And in Chronicles (I Chronicles 13:6): \"to bring up from there the Ark of the Elohim, YHWH who sits upon the cherubim, upon which the name is called\" — for HaShem sits upon the cherubim."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The abbreviation דהי\"א refers to דברי הימים א (I Chronicles); דהי\"ב refers to דברי הימים ב (II Chronicles). The citation marked להלן פסוק כ\"א refers to Ramban's own commentary later in the same chapter (Exodus 25:21)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:16:16.053338303Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}